      "text": "To convert the point \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = \\left( 3, \\frac{5 \\pi}{12}, 0 \\right)\\) from spherical coordinates to rectangular coordinates, we use the following transformations:\n\n\\[\nx = \\rho \\sin \\phi \\cos \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\rho \\sin \\phi \\sin \\theta\n\\]\n\\[\nz = \\rho \\cos \\phi\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\rho = 3, \\quad \\theta = \\frac{5 \\pi}{12}, \\quad \\phi = 0\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(\\sin \\phi\\), \\(\\cos \\phi\\), and \\(\\sin \\theta\\).\n\n### Step 1: Calculate \\(\\sin \\phi\\)\n\\[\n\\phi = 0 \\implies \\sin \\phi = \\sin 0 = 0\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate \\(\\cos \\phi\\)\n\\[\n\\phi = 0 \\implies \\cos \\phi = \\cos 0 = 1\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate \\(\\sin \\theta\\) and \\(\\cos \\theta\\)\n\\[\n\\theta = \\frac{5 \\pi}{12} \\implies \\sin \\theta = \\sin \\left( \\frac{5 \\pi}{12} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos \\theta = \\cos \\left( \\frac{5 \\pi}{12} \\right)\n\\]\n\nUsing the angle addition formulas:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( \\frac{5 \\pi}{12} \\right) = \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos \\left( \\frac{5 \\pi}{12} \\right) = \\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right) = \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{6} - \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\quad \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\quad \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}, \\quad \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( \\frac{5 \\pi}{12} \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos \\left( \\frac{5 \\pi}{12} \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these into the rectangular coordinates formulas:\n\n### Step 4: Calculate \\(x\\)\n\\[\nx = \\rho \\sin \\phi \\cos \\theta = 3 \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} = 0\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Calculate \\(y\\)\n\\[\ny = \\rho \\sin \\phi \\sin \\theta = 3 \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} = 0\n\\]\n\n### Step 6: Calculate \\(z\\)\n\\[\nz = \\rho \\cos \\phi = 3 \\cdot 1 = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the rectangular coordinates are:\n\\[\n(x, y, z) = \\left( 0, 0, 3 \\right)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the point in rectangular coordinates is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, 0, 3)}\n\\]",
